---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { supportLinks, withBase } from '@/data/site';
---
<BaseLayout title="SiteRadian security — Scoped AI access for WordPress" description="How SiteRadian handles tokens, scopes, capabilities, approvals, protection modes, audit logs, rollback, and external AI relationships.">
  <header class="page-hero"><div class="container narrow"><p class="eyebrow">Security & trust</p><h1>Designed around bounded authority.</h1><p class="lede">SiteRadian gives an assistant an operational path into WordPress without pretending that every request, credential, or change deserves equal trust.</p></div></header>
  <section class="section"><div class="container"><div class="section-header"><div><p class="eyebrow">Control model</p><h2>Four independent layers.</h2><p>A request must pass each applicable control before execution. Human approval does not replace permission, and permission does not remove approval.</p></div></div><div class="flow-grid"><article class="flow-card"><span class="flow-number"></span><h3>Token scope</h3><p>Read-only is enforced before a write operation reaches dispatch.</p></article><article class="flow-card"><span class="flow-number"></span><h3>Capability</h3><p>The credential must hold the named permission mapped to the operation.</p></article><article class="flow-card"><span class="flow-number"></span><h3>Protection</h3><p>Risk and operating mode determine whether work waits for a person.</p></article><article class="flow-card"><span class="flow-number"></span><h3>Destructive guard</h3><p>Permanent deletion requires explicit target, phrase, reason, and verified backup.</p></article><article class="flow-card"><span class="flow-number"></span><h3>Execution record</h3><p>Structured history and redacted audit context preserve what occurred.</p></article></div></div></section>
  <section class="section soft"><div class="container split"><div><p class="eyebrow">Credentials</p><h2 class="content-heading">Reveal once. Store a hash.</h2><p>SiteRadian access tokens are shown only when created. The complete value cannot be recovered later because SiteRadian retains a hash rather than the raw credential.</p><ul class="trust-list"><li>Optional expiry and creating-user context</li><li>Immediate revocation on the next request</li><li>Read-only or Full scope</li><li>23 narrower capabilities</li></ul></div><div class="boundary-card"><p class="eyebrow">Credential hygiene</p><h3>If a token leaks, revoke it.</h3><ul><li>Never put real tokens in source control.</li><li>Keep token-bearing commands out of shared logs.</li><li>Do not save terminal tokens in shell profiles by default.</li><li>Create separate access for separate clients and purposes.</li></ul></div></div></section>
  <section class="section"><div class="container split"><div><p class="eyebrow">Least necessary access</p><h2 class="content-heading">Avoid sharing server credentials when the work does not need them.</h2><p>For supported SiteRadian operations, an assistant can use a scoped SiteRadian credential instead of receiving direct SSH, FTP, or SFTP credentials. That reduces unnecessary credential exposure while keeping WordPress permission and SiteRadian governance checks in the path.</p><p>This is not a claim that SiteRadian is “more secure than SSH.” It means matching access to the work: server-level tasks still belong to appropriately managed server or hosting access.</p><a class="text-link" href={withBase('/use-cases/wordpress-admin-only/')}>Explore the admin-only workflow</a></div><div class="boundary-card"><p class="eyebrow">Bounded by design</p><h3>Only supported WordPress operations.</h3><ul><li>SiteRadian scope and capability</li><li>WordPress authorization</li><li>Protection and approval</li><li>Hosting and PHP limitations</li></ul></div></div></section>
  <section class="section soft"><div class="container split"><div><p class="eyebrow">Approval</p><h2 class="content-heading">A person decides consequential work.</h2><p>Standard protection gates medium, high, and critical changes. Strict approval gates every write. Development bypasses approval only for local and staging environments.</p><p>An assistant cannot approve its own MCP request. Undo is itself a governed change.</p><a class="text-link" href={withBase('/docs/approvals/')}>Understand approvals</a></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Audit & rollback</p><h2 class="content-heading">A record, not a vague promise.</h2><p>Changes keeps structured operation context and rollback handles. Secret patterns are redacted before audit context is written. Supported undo is field-scoped and drift-aware.</p><a class="text-link" href={withBase('/docs/rollback/')}>Understand rollback</a></div></div></section>
  <section class="section"><div class="container split"><div class="boundary-card"><p class="eyebrow">What leaves your site</p><h3>Nothing by default.</h3><ul><li>No product analytics or telemetry.</li><li>No licence check or phone-home request.</li><li>Assistant traffic is initiated by the client you configure.</li><li>Built-in AI contacts only the provider and feature you explicitly configure.</li></ul></div><div><p class="eyebrow">What SiteRadian is not</p><h2 class="content-heading">Boundaries are part of trust.</h2><ul class="trust-list"><li>Not an AI model or AI provider</li><li>Not a hosting provider</li><li>Not unrestricted remote control</li><li>Not a complete backup system</li><li>Not a replacement for WordPress roles, updates, backups, or server security</li></ul></div></div></section>
  <section class="section"><div class="container availability"><div class="availability-inner"><div><p class="eyebrow">Responsible disclosure</p><h2>Found a security issue?</h2><p>Do not open a public issue containing exploit details, private site data, or credentials. Use GitHub’s private vulnerability reporting flow so the report reaches the maintainer without public disclosure.</p></div><a class="button" href={supportLinks.privateSecurityReport}>Report privately</a></div></div></section>
</BaseLayout>
